{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md; sed -n '385,420p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/module_discovery.go; sed -n '432,475p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go; rg -n 'locale|untranslated|plumbing' .pose/knowledge; git rev-parse HEAD; git diff --stat 26b011d774c6fcab4bf6cb03f75a01e2f197921e b5fb63361f9bef4913779097cbf761da145bd997\"","aggregated_output":"# Rule: Backend Go\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for HTTP handlers, domain services, persistence, concurrency, and integrations between Go services.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Handle and propagate errors with enough context for diagnosis.\n- Validate handler input and return HTTP status codes consistent with the contract.\n- Keep business rules in service or domain layers, not in transport code.\n- Honor `context.Context` for timeouts and cancellation.\n- Put data access behind clear interfaces that support testing.\n- Use structured logs without exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Ignoring error returns, including `_` for critical errors.\n- Using `panic` in ordinary request flows instead of controlled error handling.\n- Coupling handlers directly to concrete repository implementations without an abstraction.\n- Running unbounded or unpaginated queries in potentially large endpoints.\n- Introducing concurrency without adequate synchronization or race protection.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `go test ./...` in the affected scope.\n- Run `go test -race ./...` when concurrency changes.\n- Run `go vet ./...` without blocking findings.\n- Run the applicable Go lint check, such as `golangci-lint`, without critical errors.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t\tparent := filepath.Dir(dir)\n\t\tif parent == dir || !pathWithin(boundary, parent) {\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t\tdir = parent\n\t}\n\n\t// No repository marker was found in the authorized project root. A\n\t// captured Git query distinguishes a repository rooted within the view\n\t// from a valid enclosing repository outside the view; the latter is\n\t// unknown because its worktree is outside the operation's authorized\n\t// input boundary.\n\ttop, gitErr := CaptureGovernanceGitIn(files, root, producer, 1024, \"rev-parse\", \"--show-toplevel\")\n\tif gitErr != nil {\n\t\tvar exit *exec.ExitError\n\t\tif errors.As(gitErr, &exit) && strings.Contains(strings.ToLower(string(exit.Stderr)), \"not a git repository\") {\n\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t}\n\t\tview.markUnknown(producer, \"Git context could not be proved at the project root\")\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\ttopPath := filepath.Clean(strings.TrimSpace(string(top)))\n\tif !pathWithin(boundary, topPath) {\n\t\tview.markUnknown(producer, \"Git repository root is outside the project read-view root\")\n\t}\n\treturn true\n}\n\nfunc gitMetadataAtBoundedIn(files Files, dir string) (bool, error) {\n\tgitPath := filepath.Join(dir, \".git\")\n\tif _, err := files.Lstat(gitPath); err == nil {\n\t\t// A directory or gitfile is enough to prove that this path intended to\n\t\t// carry Git metadata. Git commands will report corruption instead of\n\t\t// letting the index treat it as a non-Git checkout.\n\ntype releaseTagObject struct {\n\tcommit    string\n\tannotated bool\n\terr       error\n}\n\nfunc gitOutput(root string, args ...string) (string, error) {\n\treturn gitOutputIn(root, nil, args...)\n}\n\n// gitOutputUntranslated runs git in the C locale, for the callers that read what git says: a\n// translated git words the same answer differently.\nfunc gitOutputUntranslated(root string, args ...string) (string, error) {\n\treturn gitOutputIn(root, []string{\"LC_ALL=C\"}, args...)\n}\n\nfunc gitOutputIn(root string, env []string, args ...string) (string, error) {\n\tcmd := RawHistoryGit(root, args...)\n\tif env != nil {\n\t\tcmd.Env = append(cmd.Env, env...)\n\t}\n\tvar stderr bytes.Buffer\n\tcmd.Stderr = &stderr\n\tout, err := cmd.Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", &gitCommandError{Op: args[0], Stderr: strings.TrimSpace(stderr.String()), Err: err}\n\t}\n\tif strings.TrimSpace(stderr.String()) != \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\", &gitCommandError{Op: args[0], Stderr: strings.TrimSpace(stderr.String()), Err: errors.New(\"Git reported an incomplete or uncertain result\")}\n\t}\n\treturn string(out), nil\n}\n\n// ListReleaseTags preserves warnings about broken refs as incomplete inventory.\n// Git may omit a broken loose tag while returning success; absence cannot be inferred.\nfunc ListReleaseTags(root string) (string, error) {\n\treturn gitOutput(root, \"tag\", \"--list\", \"v*\")\n}\n\n// gitTreeEntry looks one path up in a commit's tree. found=false with a nil error is the tree\n// proving the path is not there; any failure to read the tree is an error, never absence.\nfunc gitTreeEntry(root, commit, rel string) (mode, kind, object string, found bool, err error) {\n\tout, err := gitOutput(root, \"ls-tree\", \"-z\", commit, \"--\", rel)\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:43:algum) ganhou cobertura completa contra instância populada (locale pt-BR,\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:72:locale (templates default en `knowledge.md`/`doc-audit-report.md` estavam\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:74:a convenção de path do overlay de locale (`install.go`: templates agora\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:75:seguem o mesmo padrão `locales/<locale>/.pose/templates/...` de